GPU resource metadata registry and typed resource wrappers
=========================================================

Source proposal: [Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuResourceHandles.Proposal.md)
Status: Phases 1–7 complete after delegated validation, second review and independent audit.
Phases 8–9 remain open. Retained CommandPublication Phase 9 records foundation evidence and limits.
Persistent consumer integration and integrated qualification are verified with the recorded evidence limits.
Phase 8 is a separately requested follow-on RHI design; it does not authorize runtime migration.

Planning authority
  The governing contracts record the agreed manager-owned metadata direction. The linked proposal,
  inventory and API matrix govern retained contracts. The approved CommandPublication proposal refines
  creation and execution/publication mechanics; its checklist owns reconciliation of those mechanics
  before implementation. Existing runtime behavior is not evidence that this refinement is implemented.

Goal and governing contracts
  Define one canonical metadata struct for each resource kind. GpuResourceManager owns the common
  registry of native resource metadata within its context lifetime. Key entries by resource kind and
  native-width ID through a readonly GpuResourceHandle value in a manager-owned ConcurrentDictionary.
  Entries retain canonical metadata and concurrent weak recipients; no context-generation or incarnation key.
  Deletion removes the entry and invalidates its associated wrappers. Reused IDs receive fresh entries.
  Context destruction retires the manager's registry and associations together.
  StateCache forwards observed metadata-altering commands to GpuResourceManager, which updates the
  canonical metadata and publishes the resulting typed snapshot to every associated resource wrapper.
  GpuResource<TMetadata> : GpuResource owns a private snapshot with protected ref readonly metadata
  access. Its public HandleMetadataChanged(TMetadata data) implementation assigns the complete snapshot.
  Resource abstractions expose native metadata through getter-only accessors; only manager publication
  calls the notification handler. StateCache executes metadata-changing commands and forwards their
  actual results. Wrappers never edit snapshots, construct candidates or initiate mutation publication.
  Creation requests and allocation policy remain separate from native facts; initial acquisition and
  image projection are manager-owned, including context-local tools without a registry. The manager owns canonical state; wrapper structs are synchronized snapshots.
  The manager owns metadata availability, revisions, subscription membership and retirement state.
  GpuResource<TMetadata> only stores and assigns typed metadata snapshots; it has no tracking state
  machine or lifecycle notification handlers. Non-generic GpuResource retains native identity,
  ownership and lifecycle only. Metadata-dependent validation queries the manager's original association.
  No registry-entry or weak recipient-slot back-reference is stored in wrappers.
  Retain wrapper-local ownership, disposal, subresource selection and authored policy outside native
  metadata structs. Retain existing ownership and deletion scheduling responsibilities.
  GpuResourceHandle is only the kind/ID value key. The manager entry supplies shared registration state;
  no separate owning or lifetime-bearing handle object is required.
  Preserve existing context/thread restrictions, ownership, guard rules, scheduling, render order,
  shader algorithms, content readiness and frame qualification. Reuse existing guard enforcement.
  No GpuResourceLifetime, dedicated borrowed-resource registry, duplicate binding/validation cache,
  shared-context namespace generalization, or producer teardown hook duplicating GL notifications.
  Track resource metadata, not resource contents. Preserve existing content-readiness mechanisms.
  Interception supports only native owners GL/Core, Ext and Arb. All other auxiliary owners are excluded.
  Apply this owner boundary to every command-family, variant and overload requirement below and to
  active comparisons against the retained baseline inventory. Feature origins and command suffixes
  do not exclude operations declared on allowed owners; direct VGE operations remain separately scoped.
  Legacy pre-OpenGL 3.0 fixed-function/compatibility facilities are excluded, including matrix stacks,
  display lists, server/client attribute stacks and legacy client-array state/default resets. Phase 4b
  removes their existing support. Commands introduced before 3.0 that remain part of modern OpenGL
  (for example texture/buffer operations and ordinary depth/blend state) remain supported; API age alone
  is not the removal criterion. This exclusion does not narrow the separate RHICommandList RHI design.
  Keep registry, common registration/dispatch, typed metadata definitions and native adapters in files
  with those respective responsibilities. Follow repository comment and region conventions.
  Use subagents for every build/test invocation. Keep source, unit, GPU integration, package and live
  evidence distinct. Never launch the game for visual acceptance. Creating this plan changes no runtime.

Resolved design decisions and implementation gates
  ☑ Map every resource kind and represented native metadata field to a canonical struct, separating
    native metadata from wrapper-local policy. Define registration, initialization and publication APIs.
  ☑ Reconcile resource kinds and represented fields from source with ALL metadata-altering GL commands
    and overloads from the API/extension specifications, regardless of current usage;
    do not equate existing EngineStateCallMap coverage with interception of all GL operations.
  ☑ Establish how command success and metadata-refresh failure affect canonical entries and wrapper
    snapshots without changing existing
    error handling; command arguments alone must not certify a rejected operation's result.
  ☑ Specify registration, pending deletion, deletion and context retirement transitions, preserving
    existing scheduling and preventing old wrappers or delayed work from affecting reused names.
    Use entry removal, association invalidation and cancellation/validation of queued work; do not
    reintroduce generation-based registry keys. Decide whether a separate handle remains useful.
  ☑ Select non-owning wrapper association and expired-association cleanup mechanics with bounded retention.
  ☑ Define where metadata revisions, dependent validation and existing alias-wide usage guards belong;
    keep the generic metadata notification implementation a typed snapshot assignment.
  ☑ Resolve unsupported external command paths before enabling persistent tracking for their consumers.
    Conservative handling is required for gaps; an unresolved required path is not completed coverage.
  Revised decisions must be recorded in the proposal, inventory and API coverage matrix as applicable.
  Command support itself remains implementation work in later phases.
  These decisions must satisfy the governing contracts above. Any further required scope change must be reported
  for explicit review rather than silently weakening acceptance or broadening the architecture.

