Phase 2: Establish canonical metadata and the manager registry
  Depends on Phase 1.
  Outcome: typed metadata storage, registration and publication primitives are testable before migration.

  ☑ Define the canonical metadata structs approved in Phase 1, with native metadata separated from
    ownership, content and wrapper-local policy. Preserve native-width IDs.
  ☑ Add GpuResourceManager registry lookup/acquisition, association removal and context retirement.
    Live aliases share a canonical entry; deletion removes it; reused IDs create fresh entries.
    Scope the registry to the manager's context lifetime; keys contain resource kind and ID only.
    Initial external acquisition validates resource kind and metadata under the established coverage
    contract, without requiring producer-specific allocation certificates.
  ☑ Implement non-owning wrapper associations, explicit unregister and expired-association cleanup.
    Ensure wrapper collection does not discard canonical metadata for a still-tracked native object.
  ☑ Implement manager-owned typed metadata updates and snapshot publication, including initial
    registration and the agreed unavailable-metadata behavior. Refresh canonical data once per change
    and distribute it; wrappers must not independently query GL to refresh their snapshots.
  ☑ Introduce GpuResource<TMetadata> with a private snapshot, protected read-only access and public
    HandleMetadataChanged(TMetadata data) assignment implementation.
  ☑ Implement entry-owned metadata/storage revisions and existing alias-wide usage-guard state.
    Keep registration internal to the manager; GpuResourceHandle carries only the concurrent map key.
  ☑ Have subagents verify canonical entry sharing, kind/context separation, native-width IDs, snapshot
    publication, name reuse, non-owning registration, cleanup and context retirement.
  ☑ Completion: primitives satisfy identity/retention contracts; existing wrappers remain operational.
    New tracking is not advertised to consumers before lifecycle and command coverage are integrated.

  Initial foundation qualification: second review and independent completion audit passed. Release build passed; 124 related tests passed
  (105 existing and 19 new), with no failures or skips. Canonical Release SPIR-V packaging passed;
  the packaged runtime matches the tested DLL and all 761 packaged shaders match tested assets.
  Receipts: [tests](../artifacts/gpu-resource-metadata-phase2/regression-final-v3.trx),
  [build](../artifacts/gpu-resource-metadata-phase2/build-final-v3.log),
  [package](../artifacts/gpu-resource-metadata-phase2/package-final-v2.log),
  [identity](../artifacts/gpu-resource-metadata-phase2/identity-final-v3.json).

  Concurrent-container revision: one ConcurrentDictionary keyed by the kind/ID GpuResourceHandle value
  replaces per-kind maps and a registry-wide lock. Entries retain canonical metadata and a concurrent
  weak resource recipient set; typed/image dispatch needs no association objects or projection delegates.
  Identity lookup and recipient removal support concurrent callers; native acquisition, recipient
  initialization and metadata publication retain originating-context thread affinity.
  Release SPIR-V build passed; 126 related tests passed with no failures or skips. The three concurrent
  lookup/removal/publication/retirement tests also passed five repetitions (15 test executions).
  Receipts: [build](../artifacts/gpu-resource-concurrent-registry/build-final.log),
  [tests](../artifacts/gpu-resource-concurrent-registry/regression-final.trx).
  The package/identity receipts above qualify the initial foundation candidate, before this revision.

  Earlier manager-notification correction (wrapper-local state superseded below): resources retain neither registration references nor weak recipient
  slots. The manager pushes initial/changed metadata, recoverable unavailability, revision snapshots and
  terminal retirement. Old or delayed availability notifications cannot revive retired wrappers.
  Release SPIR-V build passed; 128 related tests passed with no failures or skips. Six concurrent and
  retirement cases passed ten repetitions (60 test executions).
  Receipts: [build](../artifacts/gpu-resource-notifications/build.log),
  [tests](../artifacts/gpu-resource-notifications/related.trx).

  Earlier generic metadata boundary correction (tracking responsibilities superseded below): only GpuResource<TMetadata> owns metadata snapshots,
  availability, revisions, selected-image metadata and manager notification handlers. Manager entries
  dispatch through an internal recipient interface implemented only by the generic class.
  Non-generic GpuResource has no metadata members or notification contract.
  Release SPIR-V build passed with 0 errors; 128 related tests passed with no failures or skips,
  including retired validity through a non-generic resource reference.
  Receipts: [build](../artifacts/generic-metadata-boundary/build.log),
  [tests](../artifacts/generic-metadata-boundary/related.trx).

  Manager-owned tracking correction: GpuResource<TMetadata> retains the snapshot field and assignment
  handler, with no subscription state machine, availability/revision copies, metadata validity override
  or lifecycle notification interface. Manager entries own recipient membership and successful snapshot
  delivery; canonical availability, revisions and retirement remain entry-owned. A manager-owned weak-key
  association table preserves original registration identity without retaining wrappers or storing
  back-references in them. Metadata-dependent validation queries this original association, so direct
  snapshot assignment and native ID reuse cannot revive retired associations.
  MetadataImageSelection remains temporary scaffolding until the explicit Phase 3 migration below.
  Release SPIR-V build passed with 0 errors; 128 related tests passed with no failures or skips.
  Six concurrency/retirement cases also passed five repetitions (30 additional test executions).
  Receipts: [build](../artifacts/manager-metadata-tracking/build.log),
  [tests](../artifacts/manager-metadata-tracking/related.trx).

Phase 3: Integrate wrapper ownership and native retirement
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: existing resource implementations use typed metadata snapshots with correct ownership and deletion.

  ☑ Migrate every existing resource abstraction to GpuResource<TMetadata> using its canonical struct.
    Replace duplicated native metadata fields with accessors over the inherited metadata snapshot.
    Retain wrapper-local disposal, ownership, subresource selection and authored policy. Initialize
    snapshots from manager publication; validate availability, revisions and retirement through manager
    APIs. Resolve shared guards and membership through manager APIs without storing
    registration or weak recipient-slot references in wrappers.
    Preserve initialization of CPU declarations and existing originating-context restrictions.
    Enforce read-only derived snapshot access. Remove metadata setters and wrapper-authored capture/
    publication paths; execute wrapper mutations through StateCache and publish actual results through
    the manager. Keep allocation requests separate and verify managed and context-local acquisition.
  ☑ Move MetadataImageSelection out of GpuResource<TMetadata> into the image-specific wrapper
    (GpuFramebufferAttachment) during wrapper migration. Its current placement is temporary foundation
    scaffolding, not the intended generic-base contract. Neither GpuResource nor GpuResource<TMetadata>
    should retain image-specific selection fields or dependencies after this migration.
    Update manager image association/projection to read mip/layer/cube-face/layered selection from the
    image-specific wrapper, and adapt the image projection fixtures to that wrapper boundary.
    Verify canonical backing metadata still produces the correct selected-image snapshot and that
    unsupported selections remain unavailable without affecting other aliases.
  ☑ Integrate alias-wide pass/lifetime guard state at the location agreed in Phase 1; reuse current checks and
    release paths. Verify references cannot bypass existing guard rules by using another wrapper.
  ☑ Integrate GpuResourceManager scheduling: mark retiring allocations unavailable to managed use,
    associate queued work with its original registration and reject stale deletion of replacement objects.
    Preserve eventual native deletion and its binding effects in the originating context.
  ☑ Route every in-scope resource kind's deletion through StateCache into manager retirement, including
    current direct-GL deletion branches. Extend exact native adapters and ALL applicable overload/list/extension paths.
    Preserve existing error handling and command ordering; do not assume whole-list rollback.
  ☑ Preserve borrowed disposal as association/local-state retirement only. Preserve Detach as ownership
    transfer with surviving registration; borrowed transfer remains rejected. Observed external
    deletion must prevent later owning-wrapper disposal from deleting a reused name.
  ☑ Retain resource-specific texture/image/boundary retirement and actual implicit-unbinding behavior.
    Remove canonical entries and invalidate associated wrappers on deletion. Context retirement must
    retire its registry and must not issue deletion or restore names into another context.
  ☑ Have subagents test owning/borrowed aliases, repeated disposal, external deletion, same-name reuse,
    queued deletion, detach, guards, context retirement and unaffected bindings/restoration.
    Exercise native deletion through actual EngineStateCallMap redirection, not only direct fixtures.
  ☑ Completion: all inventoried lifecycle paths pass; no parallel borrowed lifetime mechanism exists.
    Image selection belongs to the image-specific wrapper; both resource bases are image-independent.
    Persistent consumer metadata remains gated on the next phase's complete mutation coverage.

  Architectural review corrections (required before Phase 3 completion)
  ☑ Reject Detach/ReleaseHandle when the original manager association has retired, including external
    deletion, pending retirement, context retirement and same-name reuse. Preserve successful live
    ownership transfer and borrowed-transfer rejection. Verify no stale nonzero handle escapes.
    Preserve empty CPU-declaration transfer and repeated local disposal/transfer after ownership has
    already transferred, even when another alias subsequently retains pass or lifetime guards.
  ☑ Propagate failed canonical refresh from ObserveWrapperMetadata instead of returning successful
    wrapper mutation with an unavailable old snapshot. Preserve unavailable state and native errors;
    never retry native allocation because publication failed. Verify native capacity changes while
    an injected reader failure leaves old data unavailable and produces an explicit failure outcome.
  ☑ Make buffer, texture, framebuffer and view metadata acquisition capability-aware end to end.
    Bound allocation fallbacks must not require DSA-only queries. Use existing binding scopes and
    cached capabilities; verify restored state and metadata on supported DSA and bound paths, and
    distinguish forced-path tests on DSA hardware from actual hardware without DSA.
  ☑ Preserve pending Storage effects when a grouped update is interrupted. Carry the group's change
    classification into manager invalidation so recovery advances StorageRevision as well as metadata
    revision. Verify an accepted partial image replacement followed by abandonment and recovery.
  ☑ Reconcile GpuBufferTexture factories with GpuBufferView initialization: issue the association once
    through the common native command route, preserve DSA/bound selection and native-width ranges,
    and avoid unconditional int narrowing after successful DSA setup. Cover both public factory types.
  ☑ Remove independent metadata acquisition from GpuFramebufferAttachment factories and their managed
    view path. Have manager acquisition/projection provide facts for image-selection validation;
    reuse managed canonical entries and preserve conservative external refresh until Phase 4 coverage.
  ☑ Replace native limit queries in metadata readers with the existing GpuSupport/GpuLimits cached
    limits, including texture dimensions and maximum framebuffer attachments.
  Second architectural review corrections
  ☑ Enforce existing retirement guards before native-name replacement, including lifetime references
    held by another alias. GpuTexture replacement must not drop its original association and delete
    the object after checking only storage mutability. Preserve allowed same-object storage updates;
    verify owner/alias lifetime borrows reject replacement before any deletion or association change.
  ☑ Make canonical texture acquisition handle buffer-texture metadata consistently with GpuBufferView.
    An incompatible generic wrapper acquisition must not substitute an incomplete reader or damage
    an existing buffer-view entry. Reject unsupported wrapper targets before shared refresh, or acquire
    the complete canonical target-specific facts. Verify whole/range descriptors, alias availability,
    and native error state after failed and supported acquisitions; do not invent missing fields.
  ☑ Make owning factories exception-safe across newly added registration/acquisition steps. Audit
    buffer factories (including GpuVbo and GpuUniformBuffer) and the other migrated owning factories:
    if construction fails after native creation, retire the captured allocation through StateCache
    in its originating context, remove associated registry state, and preserve the original exception.
    Test acquisition/registration failure cleanup without deleting borrowed or replacement resources.

  Third architectural review corrections
  ☑ Reject acquisition of shaders and programs already marked for deferred native deletion; native
    existence alone must not admit a fresh live registration for a retired object. Preserve deferred
    destruction and fresh registration of genuinely reused names without adding generation keys.
    Verify attached shaders and current programs through deletion request, attempted reacquisition,
    physical destruction and subsequent name reuse. The retained shader probe does not qualify programs.
  ☑ Invalidate tracked dependencies after observed backing-object deletion even when that backing
    object has no registry entry to remove. Move dependency reconciliation outside successful entry
    removal while preserving per-object deletion outcomes and native retained-storage semantics.
    Verify raw/unregistered and registered buffer backing for texture views, plus texture/renderbuffer
    backing for framebuffer descriptors; do not equate name retirement with immediate storage loss.
  ☑ Reconcile borrowed Detach/ReleaseHandle semantics across the proposal, checklist and inventory,
    explicitly resolving the renderbuffer inventory's preserve-current-transfer wording against the
    borrowed-transfer rejection contract before changing runtime behavior. Apply the resolved contract
    consistently to borrowed framebuffer/renderbuffer wrappers and existing texture/image borrowers.
    Verify both transfer APIs, live owning transfer, and that borrowed disposal or rejected transfer
    cannot delete the external owner's resource. Do not label preserved legacy behavior a new regression.

  ☑ Have subagents convert the retained defect probes into correctness regressions, run affected
    lifecycle/publication/fallback checks, and recheck these fixes against the proposal and downstream
    obligations before restoring completion. Existing passing receipts do not close these findings.
  Fourth architectural review corrections
  ☑ Reject borrowed framebuffer identity replacement while that wrapper retains lifetime references,
    before changing routing, attachment state or its manager association. Preserve same-identity
    metadata refresh and allow replacement after release; verify old/new entry guard accounting.
  ☑ Retain deferred disposal requests rejected before retirement by local or alias-wide usage guards.
    Bound each drain to its initial admissions so blocked resources cannot spin or starve later work.
    Verify deletion after release, duplicate admission, failure isolation and terminal queue closure.
  ☑ Give complete metadata readers responsibility for native validity validation and remove redundant
    manager-side prechecks on acquisition/refresh. Preserve reserved-name buffer initialization and
    invalid/deletion-pending rejection; verify validation counts and unavailable metadata behavior.
  ☑ Convert both retained review probes to correctness regressions and run focused lifecycle,
    registry and metadata acquisition/publication tests before closing these corrections.

  Fifth architectural review corrections (required before restoring Phase 3 completion)
  ☑ Make texture metadata parameter queries capability-aware independently of DSA availability.
    Gate immutable-storage and texture-view parameters on their actual core/extension support;
    TEXTURE_IMMUTABLE_LEVELS requires texture-view support, not just OpenGL 4.2 texture storage.
    Preserve valid ordinary texture acquisition on older supported contexts using cached capabilities
    and supported native facts, without speculative queries or invented metadata. Verify supported
    bound/DSA paths, error-queue preservation and binding restoration; distinguish forced capability
    tests on newer hardware from execution on genuinely older contexts.
  ☑ Correct context-local GpuTextureView source validation so metadata-only snapshot replacement does
    not imply storage replacement. Preserve view validity after an unchanged SetMipRange call without
    an installed manager, while still rejecting actual source retirement or allocation replacement.
    Keep dependency state image-specific and manager-owned when tracking is available; do not add
    tracking state to either generic resource base or introduce a parallel lifetime registry.
  ☑ Propagate authoritative metadata-publication failures from GpuTexture.SetMipRange rather than
    swallowing them in its catch-all handler. Preserve unavailable canonical state and existing native
    error handling, without retrying the native command because publication failed. Verify successful
    publication, injected reader failure and a recoverable subsequent refresh through the public API.
  ☑ Remove duplicate validity and view-range queries from ConfigureTextureView. Acquire one complete
    validated texture snapshot and compare its view fields against the requested range before
    publication. Preserve failed-creation cleanup and rejection of unacceptable native ranges;
    verify a single validity check and one read of each view parameter on bound and named paths.
  ☑ Have subagents convert the two retained defect probes into correctness regressions, run affected
    texture/view, acquisition, publication-failure and lifecycle tests, and run the canonical build.
    Reconcile the fixes with the proposal, inventory and downstream phases; complete source review
    and independent audit before restoring completion. Prior passing receipts do not close these tasks.

  Eighth architectural review corrections
  ☑ Allow mutable framebuffer configuration repair without validating invalid outgoing images.
    Preserve guards, native-result publication and pending reattachment of other dirty slots;
    reject invalid images when rendering, while allowing incremental removal or replacement.
  ☑ Gate managed texture-view use on manager-owned availability of both the view and its original
    source dependency. Preserve metadata-only recovery and storage-change invalidation; add no
    metadata tracking to either resource base or parallel registry.
  ☑ Add regression coverage for removal/replacement, other pending attachments, guards and binding
    restoration, unavailable view/source metadata and recovery. Run focused tests and a forced
    Release/SPIR-V build through a subagent; reconcile documentation and review before closure.

