Phase 7: Design and implement the unified GpuTextureRef API
  Depends on Phase 6's qualified resource, metadata and binding contracts.
  Outcome: owned textures, borrowed textures and native texture views expose a common non-owning
  texture-reference API for binding and validation, without changing native storage or ownership.
  Approved design: [Rendering.GpuTextureRef.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuTextureRef.Proposal.md).
  Status: complete after implementation, delegated validation, second review and independent audit.
  A readonly value retains one private GpuResource<GpuTextureMetadata> reference and follows the wrapper's
  current native object. No public lifecycle methods, underlying-resource accessor or captured identity token.

  ☑ Design GpuTextureRef and record its contract in a focused proposal before implementation.
    Inventory GpuTexture, BorrowedTexture, GpuTextureView, GpuTextureBinding, framebuffer attachment
    selection, ShaderInputValidation, ShaderPreparedSubmission and generated shader bindings.
    Define the representation, construction, empty-reference behavior, equality and access to resource
    identity, target, available metadata and view-relative subresources. Resolve class/value/interface
    choices from these requirements rather than introducing a second resource or metadata hierarchy.
  ☑ Define reference lifetime and retention explicitly: retain the managed wrapper and follow its
    intentional native-object replacement. Preserve manager-owned borrower retirement, deletion/name-reuse
    safety, context retirement and native-view source dependencies. Obtaining or releasing
    a reference must never transfer ownership or delete the native resource.
    Keep registration, availability, revisions and snapshot publication manager-owned; do not add
    another lifetime registry, metadata snapshot authority or binding/validation cache.
  ☑ Separate the consumer-facing binding/validation contract from ownership and mutation operations.
    Preserve allocation, uploads, storage changes, disposal and Detach on the appropriate existing
    resource APIs. Borrowing remains valid for mutable external textures; obtaining GpuTextureRef
    must not create a GL texture view, allocate native storage or query already-available metadata.
    Preserve explicit image level/layer/access/format and framebuffer attachment selection rather
    than conflating a managed reference with a native texture view or an image-binding descriptor.
  ☑ Walk owned, borrowed and native-view examples through the proposed API, including producer
    replacement, unavailable metadata/recovery and shared-storage alias validation. Review the design
    with the user and record approval before implementing the selected representation.
  ☑ Implement GpuTextureRef and conversions/accessors from GpuTexture (including BorrowedTexture)
    and GpuTextureView through their existing manager associations. Keep focused source ownership
    and preserve existing context/thread, pass-usage, content-readiness and native deletion contracts.
    Declare implicit conversion operators on GpuTexture and GpuTextureView, using an internal
    GpuTextureRef(GpuResource<GpuTextureMetadata>?) constructor. BorrowedTexture uses its base conversion;
    null produces an empty reference. Add no reverse conversion or public From factory.
    Store only the private GpuResource<GpuTextureMetadata> reference; expose no public underlying-resource
    accessor or lifecycle/mutation methods. Consolidate remaining GpuTextureView usability checks in
    GpuResourceManager, including local-tool handling, without recursive wrapper validity calls.
  ☑ Migrate binding/validation entry points, generated shader-binding support and their consumers
