Phase 8: Design the RHICommand and RHICommandList render hardware interface
  Depends on Phase 7 and its implemented GpuTextureRef API, together with Phase 6's qualified contracts.
  Outcome: a standalone, reviewable design for an RHI-style command system, with the OpenGL backend
  encapsulating native execution and subsequent OpenGlStateCache updates. This phase produces a proposal,
  not the command implementation or the StateCache rename.
  Proposal: [Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md](Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md).
  Status: proposal reviewed and approved, including naming, migration, recording and OpenGL 4.5.
  Implementation is tracked separately in Rendering.GpuCommands.todo; its first phase defines final
  contracts and complete replacement units before runtime migration. Runtime Phases 1–7 now have
  qualification receipts there, including the implemented OpenGlStateCache split; that plan's Phase 8
  corrects wrapper/helper RHI boundary violations and qualifies the native OpenTK interception
  replacement through subphase 8h. That plan's metadata lifecycle redesign (8i), combined closure (8j)
  and management naming (8k) remain open before integrated/package/user-run qualification in Phase 9.
  Design authority is the current resource/binding contracts, inventory/API coverage and archived
  proposals at 3f307365 (command publication) and b9fca04c (texture reference).
  This design task makes no runtime implementation claim; execution and cache renaming belong to that plan.

  ☑ Create Rendering.GpuCommands.Proposal.md as a self-contained design derived from this project's
    resource, execution and binding contracts. Define semantic operations and backend responsibilities
    rather than merely wrapping individual GL calls. Do not reference other engines as design authority.
  ☑ Define the responsibilities and APIs of RHICommand, RHICommandList, backend execution and
    OpenGlStateCache (the planned name for StateCache). Keep the public command model independent of
    OpenGL entry points, binding targets and overload conventions; translate backend-neutral operations
    into native commands inside the OpenGL backend. Establish extension points for other backends
    without requiring another backend implementation as part of this design.
  ☑ Define coverage for resource creation/destruction, storage changes, uploads/copies/readback,
    pipeline and resource binding, render passes, draw/dispatch, synchronization and queries.
    Use the implemented GpuTextureRef contract for texture references; specify other typed resource references,
    descriptors, capabilities and unsupported-operation behavior.
    Keep wrapper allocation policy and higher-level rendering orchestration above backend execution;
    retain manager ownership of canonical metadata and typed snapshot publication.
    Define one typed RHI resource-creation result returning a completed owned resource or diagnostics,
    not a public recipient/native-handle/nullable-metadata handoff. Keep manager initialization and
    publication mechanics internal; preserve existing resource abstractions and ownership services.
    Specify separate initial-content payloads, payload/staging lifetimes and usable-versus-GPU-complete
    semantics. Separate external borrowing and explicit ownership-taking adoption from owned creation.
    Cover candidate-only cleanup, original-context retirement, owner replacement and unchanged original
    borrower/view associations. No parallel registry or new resource-management hierarchy.
    Define shader/PSO contracts before command payload and draw/dispatch implementation: immutable
    stage-code inputs, backend-private compilation/linking, ready executable generations and prepared
    interfaces; synchronous/pending shader creation results and candidate-only failure cleanup.
    Define graphics/compute PSO descriptions, immediate realization, exact shader-generation identity,
    renderer-owned reuse without a new global cache, consumer-only references and disposal authority.
    Define atomic installation of replacement shader/pipeline sets, preservation of the installed set
    on failure, and rejection of stale recorded pipelines/binding tables without silent retargeting.
    Implement shaders/PSOs as a separate complete migration unit after the shared contracts, before
    draw/dispatch integration. No temporary current-program/GraphicsPipeline compatibility API.
    RHICommandContext entirely replaces GraphicsCommandContext for submission: migrate pass sequencing,
    pipeline/dynamic state, binding validation/publication, draw/dispatch and boundary coordination.
    Update every caller and delete GraphicsCommandContext in that replacement unit, with no forwarding
    facade or duplicated submission state. Retain specialized helpers behind the unified context and
    renderer-owned orchestration above it; immediate and recorded execution share the same machinery.
    Retain manager publication and existing native retirement; no new metadata authority. Extensive
    PSO precaching, worker compilation and parallel translation remain outside this scope.
  ☑ Require OpenGL 4.5 or newer and OpenTK.Graphics.OpenGL4 for managed RHI execution.
    Validate baseline/version and required entry points once at backend initialization; reject older
    contexts without fallback. No platform/adoption examination is required for this approved decision.
    Use core DSA wherever equivalent; remove older-context named/bound branches and redundant native
    variants for baseline operations. Keep optional features beyond 4.5 capability-gated.
    Implement baseline operations directly, without per-operation variant tables, DSA enable flags,
    version strategies or a generic fallback registry. RHICapabilities exposes semantic limits/optional
    features from existing cached discovery. Use core Create* and named queries wherever equivalent;
    retain reserved-name behavior only where the specific creation/external contract requires it.
    Callers express semantic operations without native selection or manual binding scopes. Bound APIs
    remain only where semantics require them, including mutable image definitions and exact external
    calls; preserve their surrounding state within the backend. Never retry failed native execution.
    Demonstrate buffers, textures, framebuffers and VAOs; test baseline admission/rejection, DSA state
    preservation, necessary bound semantics and optional-feature support. Publish metadata exactly once.
  ☑ Specify command-list recording, execution, submission, reset/reuse and completion semantics.
    Evaluate immediate and deferred execution, argument/data ownership, resource retention, cancellation,
    context/thread affinity and synchronization. Distinguish recording, native execution and GPU
    completion; do not assume multithreaded OpenGL execution or asynchronous submission is required.
    Approved recording contract: exclusive list ownership, list-local mutable state, frozen/copied
    arguments and no recording-time native context, resource metadata, registry or executed-cache access.
    Define closed-list publication/ownership transfer and explicit submission ordering now. Implement
    immediate and single-threaded recorded execution first; keep native execution context-affine.
    Preserve efficient execution without requiring performance baselines, benchmarks or timing studies.
    Worker integration requires separate design approval and concurrency/lifetime tests;
    a dedicated RHI execution thread and parallel native translation remain separate decisions.
  ☑ Specify the execution sequence: commands encapsulate native OpenGL calls, then invoke
    OpenGlStateCache to reconcile their actual state, metadata and deletion effects. Preserve binding
    suppression decisions before execution, rejected-command behavior, unknown-state recovery,
    grouped publication and exactly-once manager notifications. Recording alone must not change
    executed state or publish resource metadata; content updates remain distinct from metadata changes.
  ☑ Design integration with existing wrappers/upload helpers, render submission, pass scopes,
    deletion queues and EngineStateCallMap redirection. Route intercepted external GL operations into
    the same execution/observation contract without recursion, duplicate execution or duplicate
    notifications. Plan the StateCache-to-OpenGlStateCache rename and migration boundaries, preserving
    existing resource ownership, render ordering, context retirement and external-state restoration.
    Approved migration contract: define final RHI interfaces/dependency rules first, then replace each
    operation family completely, including all affected callers and blocking dependencies. Remove
    superseded APIs in the same replacement; prohibit temporary compatibility facades/forwarding shims.
    Track unmigrated families as incomplete. Verify behavior, publication, suppression/restoration and
    absence of internal execution bypasses before accepting a replacement. Exact engine adapters remain
    the permanent external boundary, not a temporary migration layer.
    Each family replacement removes obsolete baseline capability branches, retry state, DSA-emulation
    scopes and fallback-only fixtures across wrappers, helpers, creation and readers. Replace those
    fixtures with core-operation/state-preservation coverage; retain necessary bound/external tests.
  ☑ Walk representative existing paths through the proposed API: cube/mip allocation, buffer
    core DSA and required bound semantics, framebuffer reattachment failure, draw/dispatch, external deletion and queued
    resource retirement. Document error propagation and resource/argument lifetime through execution
    and GPU completion. Define focused correctness checks for storage reuse, avoidance of unnecessary
    allocations/redundant queries and preserved binding suppression; no performance measurement gate.
  ☑ Review the complete design against the implemented resource contracts and resolve conflicts.
    Present the standalone proposal for approval before deriving implementation phases or changing
    runtime code. Record unresolved design decisions explicitly.
  ☑ Completion: the RHI boundary, command/list semantics, the OpenGL 4.5 baseline and backend-owned native dispatch,
    backend execution, OpenGlStateCache integration,
    migration and validation strategy are concrete and internally consistent; design review is complete
    and the proposal is approved. Implementation and renaming remain separately authorized work.

Phase 9: Upgrade PSO resource binding to capability-selected multi-bind
  Depends on Phase 7's unified texture-reference integration and Phase 6's qualification; reconcile with the Phase 8 RHI design.
  Outcome: PSO graphics/compute submission batches eligible resource bindings when multi-bind is
  available, while preserving equivalent behavior through the existing individual-call fallback.
  This work does not require implementing the proposed RHI or renaming StateCache.

  ☐ Map PSO application, ShaderPreparedSubmission, prepared bindings and geometry binding to the
    applicable multi-bind families. Consult the Khronos GL_ARB_multi_bind/core specification and
    installed OpenTK signatures for BindTextures, BindSamplers, BindImageTextures, BindBuffersBase,
    BindBuffersRange and BindVertexBuffers. Record eligibility and semantic differences for each
    family; do not assume every individual binding can be replaced by its plural equivalent.
  ☐ Use the existing cached GpuSupport.Graphics.SupportsArbMultiBind capability (extension or core
    OpenGL 4.4). Keep capability selection independent of DSA support and preserve the current
    individual-call path when unavailable or when a binding cannot be represented equivalently.
  ☐ Form binding batches within existing submission/prepared-binding ownership. Validate complete
    inputs before execution, preserve resource lifetime and metadata checks, and retain draw/dispatch
    ordering. Group eligible slot ranges without changing untouched slots or introducing another
    binding cache. Compare changed-run batching with wider ranges using measurements; avoid recurring
    allocation where existing reusable storage can serve submission safely.
  ☐ Implement native batch execution and cache reconciliation in StateCache's owning binding modules.
    Preserve stable-binding suppression, unknown-state recovery, zero/unbind semantics, texture target
    knowledge, indexed buffer ranges, VAO-specific vertex state and boundary restoration. Account for
    per-entry native rejection rather than treating a batch as atomic or marking all requested state
    known after a nonthrowing call. Publish only represented resource metadata effects through the
    existing manager route; ordinary binding changes must not trigger unrelated metadata refreshes.
  ☐ Preserve explicit image level/layer/access/format selections and other single-call semantics that
    a batch API cannot express. Preserve fallback-visible generic buffer binding, active texture and
    VAO state wherever existing contracts require it. Reconcile existing external-call redirection
    and invalidation so batch execution cannot bypass the authoritative cache or notify twice.
  ☐ Have subagents exercise capability enabled/disabled and DSA-independent paths for graphics and
    compute submission, dense/sparse/unchanged slots, zero bindings, mixed texture targets, image
    selections, buffer ranges and vertex bindings. Verify equivalent outputs and sentinel-state
    restoration, unknown-state recovery, rejected entries, resource deletion/reuse and unavailable
    metadata. Record hardware/API cases that could not be executed.
  ☐ Have subagents measure matched individual-call and batch paths: native call counts, CPU submission
    cost, allocations and representative changed/unchanged binding patterns. Retain reproducible
    receipts and choose batching policy from the results; do not infer frame-time gains from call
    counts. Keep any measurement instrumentation temporary and remove it after qualification.
  ☐ Reconcile current PSO/cache documentation and Phase 8 design integration, run affected regression
    tests and canonical build/package verification through subagents, and independently review the
    final candidate. Preserve the existing public resource APIs and rendering behavior.
  ☐ Completion: eligible PSO bindings use multi-bind on supported contexts, fallback behavior is
    equivalent, cache/restoration and metadata contracts hold, and correctness/performance evidence
    identifies the qualified candidate and any remaining limitations.

Proposal traceability
  Intent/responsibilities: governing contracts; Phases 2–5.
  Canonical structs, manager registry and context/retention: Phases 1–3 and 6.
  Ownership, disposal, detach, guards and scheduling: Phases 1 and 3.
  Generic wrapper snapshots, metadata publication, failures and revisions: Phases 1–4.
  StateCache/native coverage and binding behavior: Phases 1, 3, 4 and 4a.
  Compiler-checked native adapters and exact signature mapping: Phase 4a.
  Legacy fixed-function/compatibility exclusions and implementation removal: Phase 4b.
  GL/Core, EXT and ARB interception-owner restriction: Phase 4c.
  Joint binding/acquisition analysis and architectural approval: Phases 4d and 4e.
  Command-publication implementation, including binding/acquisition repairs and supplied-metadata
  preparation: Phase 4f delegates to Rendering.CommandPublication.todo.
  Cloud/depth consumers and content contracts: Phase 5.
  Scope, source layout and documentation: governing contracts; Phases 2–6.
  Acceptance and measurements: baseline in Phase 1; focused verification in Phases 2–5; Phase 6.
  Separately requested unified GpuTextureRef design and implementation: Phase 7.
  Separately requested RHI command-system design and OpenGlStateCache rename planning: Phase 8.
  Separately requested capability-selected PSO multi-bind upgrade: Phase 9.

Final acceptance
  ☐ Every proposal requirement is implemented and verified, or an explicit deferral is approved with
    its rationale and consequences. Conservative handling alone does not complete a required migration.
  ☐ The context-scoped manager owns canonical metadata keyed by resource kind and native-width ID.
    Every resource abstraction uses GpuResource<TMetadata> snapshots and metadata accessors, with
    ownership and other wrapper-local policy kept separate. No duplicate authoritative metadata exists.
  ☐ Registration and retirement preserve ownership, guard, deletion, transfer and context contracts
    across aliases; releasing non-owning references never deletes native storage, deletion removes
    registry entries and stale wrappers or queued work never affect reused names.
  ☐ Metadata notifications and revision-aware validation cover the inventoried supported paths;
    native integration evidence uses actual redirection and unknown/error paths remain correct.
  ☐ Metadata interception uses compiler-checked C# adapter bodies and explicit exact signature mapping;
    runtime discovery and the call-replacement transpiler preserve the established engine boundary.
  ☐ Legacy fixed-function/compatibility support and dedicated tests are removed, with modern retained
    commands preserved and the active coverage matrix reconciled to the supported boundary.
  ☐ Common tracking supports the intended cloud/depth consumers without duplicated lifetime systems,
    while render/content behavior and existing state restoration remain correct.
  ☐ Required tests, package checks, cleanup, documentation and measured call/cost comparisons are
    complete for the qualified candidate. Live appearance and frame-time claims match actual evidence.
  ☑ Phase 7 supplies the unified GpuTextureRef binding/validation API for owned, borrowed and native-view
    inputs while preserving ownership, manager metadata authority, lifecycle and cache behavior.
  ☐ The separately requested Phase 8 command-system design is reviewed and approved; its completion
    does not claim that RHICommand, RHICommandList or the OpenGlStateCache rename are implemented.
  ☐ Phase 9 PSO multi-bind integration preserves individual-call fallback semantics and authoritative
    cache behavior, with regression, package and matched measurement evidence for the final candidate.
